# 3. SEPARATE LOGIN AND AUTHENTICATION SYSTEM

Admin, Staff, and Users must have separate login experiences.

### Admin Login

At `/myadmin`:

- Display Admin App branding.
- Display Admin App logo.
- Admin-specific login UI.
- Secure authentication.
- Admin-only session handling.
- Admin role verification from the backend.
- Redirect authenticated admins to the Admin Dashboard.

### Staff Login

At `/mystaff`:

- Display Staff App branding.
- Display Staff App logo.
- Staff-specific login UI.
- Secure authentication.
- Staff role verification from the backend.
- Redirect authenticated staff to Staff Dashboard.

### User Login

At `/`:

- Preserve existing user authentication.
- Display User App branding.
- Maintain existing login, registration, forgot-password, and reset-password functionality.

### Security requirements

- Do not trust frontend role values.
- Verify roles on the backend.
- Admin routes must reject normal users and staff.
- Staff routes must reject normal users and unauthorized admins if the role policy requires it.
- User routes must not expose administrative operations.
- Protect APIs and database operations, not only frontend routes.
- Implement secure session handling.
- Implement logout and session expiration.
- Prevent unauthorized URL access.
- Redirect unauthorized users to the correct login page.
- Never expose private credentials or service-role keys.

### Separate login does not mean duplicate authentication databases

Use the existing authentication provider and user records where appropriate.

Separate the application entry points and authentication flows while maintaining a consistent and secure identity system.

